\f2\b\fs30 \cf0 \strokec2 Your Daily Amari Method Protocols
\f1\b0\fs24 \strokec2 \

\f2\b\fs30 \strokec2 Part 1 Suspension Squat
\f1\b0\fs24 \strokec2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\fs30 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Practice for 5 minutes per day (cumulative)
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Keep feet on ground in a resting squat
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Alternate between passive and active shoulder blades
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Move slowly and intuitively
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Focus on your breath: in the nose, out the mouth\'a0
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Use sounding/vocalizing on exhale to release tension
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Rotate rings with hands to connect with natural shoulder movement
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \uc0\u8232 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs30 \cf0 \strokec2 Part 2 Suspension Squat
\f1\b0\fs24 \strokec2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\fs30 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Lower the rings a good amount, until they are at the level of the hip bone or a few inches lower
\f1\fs24 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Grab both rings & walk your feet back as far as you can & sit back into a comfortable squatting position
\f1\fs24 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Let your arms be completely straight and fall all the way back into the squat
\f1\fs24 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Let your chest fall all the way forward, which will take pressure off of your low back. The more you lower your chest, the more you can step your feet back to try to get your chest closer to being parallel to the ground
\f1\fs24 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Your whole back will feel like a suspension bridge, floating between the pressure in your hands and the weight being held all the way back in your hips
\f1\fs24 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Let your shoulder blades gently come together and rest down your back
\f1\fs24 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Practice going back-and-forth between the full Suspension Squat\'a0(part 1 above) & the Suspension Bridge & see which one speaks to you more in the moment - most people will do a combination of the 2
\f1\fs24 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Let the amount of time you do this be guided by your intuition / don\'92t exceed your limits
\f1\fs24 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Stay focused on your breath: in the nose, out the mouth & use sighs and sounds to help release any superfluous holding/tension
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\fs30 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Notice what part of your body is working too hard, and what part of your body isn\'92t working enough.
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs30 \cf0 \strokec2 Hand Balancer
\f1\b0\fs24 \strokec2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\fs30 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Squeeze each hand and open for approximately 7 seconds
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Repeat 3 times per hand, 2-3 times daily or as needed
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Keep middle knuckles slightly bent when opening to protect joints
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Try not to engage any part of your arm: wrist, elbow or shoulder. Keep the action in the hand
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 As you open your hand (into the opposing force of the other hand), the base of your thumb should be working in opposition to the base of your pinky
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Don\'92t impede the movement of the pinky. Your grip should be on the metacarpals, not the fingers
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\fs30 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Remember that the gripping hand should not overpower the hand that is opening - it should be equal and opposing forces
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs30 \cf0 \strokec2 Power Posture
\f1\b0\fs24 \strokec2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f0\fs30 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Work up to 90+ seconds, 2-3 times daily
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Stand with fingers interlaced behind back, elbows slightly bent
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Squeeze shoulder blades together as if holding a pencil between them
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Keep knees soft
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Try gently bouncing on the balls of the feet to to help the shoulder blades come together more
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs30 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Let your focus be on the sound of your breath: in the nose & out the mouth
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs30 \cf0 \strokec2 Spine Align Bridge (passive)
\f1\b0\fs24 \strokec2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f0\fs30 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Practice twice daily, approximately 10 minutes each time
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Essential practice \'96 prioritize this exercise. Your body will naturally gravitate more towards the active or passive version of this pose. For some of you a perfect 50-50 balance will be what works best\'a0
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 This pose is all about micro movements & listening to your body
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Alternate lifting one foot at a time
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Inhale as you lift your foot, exhale as you lower it
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Lift your foot as high as possible while keeping the foot and the knee relaxed\'a0\'a0
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 There are three heights to the yoga block: flat, side & tall. Stay on the flat side until you\'92re absolutely sure that your body is ready to move on
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The standard yoga block is 4 inches tall on the flat side - if that is too much there is a 3 inch block option
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Keep your arms wide with your hands just above your head & your elbows and shoulders relaxed
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs30 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 If there is any tension in your shoulders, or they can\'92t lay flat, try bending your elbows and pushing your bent elbows down into the floor (with your hands in the air). That should take the pressure off of your shoulders. Eventually, your shoulders and arms will be able to lay flat. This will be one of the ways you\'92ll know that you\'92re making progress
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs30 \cf0 \strokec2 Spine Align Bridge (active)
\f1\b0\fs24 \strokec2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\f0\fs30 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Practice 2 sets for 2-3 minutes or longer
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls6\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Make small, pulsating movements to avoid static holding
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls6\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Option: feet on foam roller for higher angle, or keep feet on floor if preferred
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls6\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Walk your shoulder blades together to create a padding of muscle around the spine
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls6\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
